{
  "image_name": "1ca8d606a0d8ae532bb8080984c27bfe.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes four genetic forms of familial hyperaldosteronism and links each to its molecular mechanism and possible treatment. Across all forms, the central theme is excess aldosterone production caused by altered regulation of adrenal steroid synthesis or ion-channel function.\n\nIn FH-I, an unequal crossover joins regulatory and coding regions of CYP11B1 and CYP11B2, creating a chimeric gene that places aldosterone synthase under ACTH control. This leads to abnormal aldosterone production in the adrenal zona fasciculata and is shown as treatable with glucocorticoids.\n\nThe other forms involve membrane proteins. FH-II shows mutations in the chloride channel CLCN2, with the diagram highlighting altered residues across the channel structure. The proposed effect is increased chloride efflux, membrane depolarization, opening of voltage-gated calcium channels, and higher aldosterone production; no targeted treatment is indicated.\n\nFH-III involves mutations in KCNJ5, a potassium channel, causing loss of potassium selectivity and sodium entry, again leading to depolarization, calcium-channel activation, and increased aldosterone synthesis. Macrolides are noted as a possible future therapy.\n\nFH-IV shows mutations in CACNA1H, a calcium channel, altering calcium current properties and raising intracellular calcium, which stimulates aldosterone production. Calcium channel blockers are suggested as a potential future treatment.",
